A||||33||LemonSky||Has anyone ever sold a Fiero for over $3000.00?||||5||
Z||000000||LemonSky||12-12-2000||11:04 PM||||Okay, I own a Gold 1986 SE6 automatic, but I'm about to move from Georgia to California, and have little choice but to sell it. I've been telling people that I'd like to get $3500, but everyone says I'm insane. Defensively, I respond by saying if you want a car for $3500, why don't you buy a bland 1989 Honda Accord with 115,000 miles on it. But with a car like the Fiero, you'll have something that garners interest and intrigue by people who recall falling in love with it when it first came out. I mean, so many people will come up to me on the street and ask about it, but for some odd reason, everyone thinks it's only worth a $1000.00. Tell me, people, is it really unreasonable to ask that much? <P>FYI, the car also has A/C (re-charged in August 2000), interior and exterior in really good condition, it's been in our family its entire 15-year life, we have all the paperwork including a rebuilt engine invoice from 1991 when the Pontiac dealership F***-up the engine when placing inthe firewall. That engine has about 100,000 miles, but in a recent tune-up, the test showed that the cylinders are compressing with excellent efficiency. In a recent Pep Boys 200-point test, everything passed, but the belts need to be replaced which I'll do soon.||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||12-12-2000||11:18 PM||||Personally, I think $3500 is too high, meaning I wouldn't pay that much, but someone else might. You don't say mileage, leads me to think it is pretty high. I personally wouldn't pay more than $1500 for almost any Fiero '88GT or '84 econo-coupe. They just aren't worth that much to me. <BR>To put it another way, I paid $1,100 for my '88 Formula 5-speed with 140K miles and was in immacualte condition when I purchased it. Paid $500 for my '86SE v-6 4-speed with 101,000 miles the other month. Needed a tune-up, fired up, and have put almost 3,000 trouble free miles on it. It is in what I call fair condition for a car, good for a 14 year old car though. I personally wouldn't pay more than $1200 for an '86SE in good shape.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever2||12-12-2000||11:29 PM||||fiero56, where were you when I did my Fiero shopping?  From now on, I am going to consult you regarding any car purchase.  Are you that shrewd with other purchases, like groceries and stuff?  I started to get the flame gun out (I paid 5 g's for my GT, and 3k for my Coupe), but I can't discount your claims if your cars are indeed that good.  Maybe I'm jealous since I never could negotiate anything (mrfiero, you didn't see that).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||12-12-2000||11:32 PM||||I sold an 88gt 2 months ago for $12,900<BR>only had 762 miles on it.<P><BR>Cheever, did you buy a fiero from mrfiero?<P>I bought my last one from him too..  Man thay guy does get around.  <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroLisa||12-12-2000||11:38 PM||||Definitely would not recommend trying to sell your Fiero to a Fiero-person. Sell it to some innocent bystander. <P>No really, they don't know how cheaply we all want to buy them, they think they are just cool looking cars and don't know. <P>I was thinking the same thing though, when I was buying my GT. I paid $4000 for a '94 Mercury Tracer. There's power and styling, let me tell you...Oh wait. That car, yeah it gets me around, and it's got only 80,000 miles, and the sad thing is- i bought it for under blue book value! It's a giant pile of automotive crap. Whereas the Fiero, though it has its flaws like many other cars, is fun and easily modifiable. (&lt;-- is that a word?)<BR>Enough of my ranting. I also don't understand why fieros are considered such cheap cars. It's fine for me, I'd be scared to see what kind of other model sports car I could get for $2300. Certainly not one that looks and runs as well as my Fiero! <P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>*Lots of New Pics!* <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-12-2000||11:41 PM||||I never bought a car from mrfiero, yet. Although I would love to get my hands on his '84 econo for $300. I have bought more stuff from Mike than from anyone else other than FS.<P>Both of my cars were advertised low. I actually never negotiated the price. The owners talked themselves down. The Formula was priced at $1250 but he said he'd take $1,100. The SE was advertised at $600, but since it wouldn't start at his place, he said $500. Turned out the brass(copper???)terminals on the dist cap had corroded to green stuff preventing spark. I never call about a Fiero unless I see a deal.<P>I see this one is still for sale:<BR><B>Not my car.</B><BR>1986 Pontiac Fiero <BR>86 PONTIAC FIERO GT, brand new tires, needs clutch cable, runs well, $400 or best offer, (614)818-9586 WORTHINGTON <BR>$400 <BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Hank is Here||12-12-2000||11:43 PM||||Well, <BR> I bought my 87 GT for 3K this year with 122XXX on it.  For the condition it was in I am very happy with it. The one thing that would bring down the price of Fiero's in general is how pratical they are for most people.  Heck a 88' Honda 4 door more people have a use for than a Fiero so that is why they might have a higher value.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cheever2||12-12-2000||11:45 PM||||No, I'm not giving him money, I want money <B>from</B> him.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Wished I cudda traded for that silver GT he just put on eBay, but no funds right now.  No, he's getting my 88 GT w/ body damage.  I hate to let her go, but she needs a better home.  If he doesn't mind me telling all to the world, he said something about having a project to keep him out of trouble.  Problem is, the car <B>runs</B>, and I couldn't resist taking it around the block now & then myself.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||LemonSky||12-12-2000||11:51 PM||||Sorry, I forgot to mention that although the engine is only 100,000 miles-old, the car has 200,000 miles overall. The KBB value is $2360, but like so many of us in this group feel about their own Fieros, I believe it's worth more. But at least I'm not desperate to sell, in fact, if I have to, I'd love to take it to California. But I'm uncomfortable about driving it that kind of distance, plus I have to lug alot of stuff with me, so I need to purchase a car with a higher trunk capacity.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||12-13-2000||12:03 AM||||GA to CA is a long drive. I just got back a few days ago from driving from OH to OR(not in a Fiero). Ever think about putting a hitch on the back and towing a smaller u-haul trailer or similar. As long as you don't <B>really</B> overload the trailer you should be fine. I know Mike trailers Fieros with his Fieros sometimes(Ed Parks has done it too).||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfiero||12-13-2000||12:04 AM||||Ross.....I guess I do get around.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   I have bought and sold more Fieros than I can remember (I lost track after 30).  Right now I have 8 Fieros, I'm selling one and buying 3 more!<P>Drew.....I still have the econo-coupe (hint hint).  As far as I know all it needs is a slave cylinder.  I removed  it and found that the piston is rusted to the cylinder.  I think I will try replacing the slave to see if that is all it needs. Of course I will have to double the price!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Cheever.....I'm not a good negotiator either.  I usually pay too much for things, although I do get a few deals every now and then.  Now let's not drive the GT anymore.....you may decide you want to keep it (and I can't have that!).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000011||Cheever2||12-13-2000||12:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>  Now let's not drive the GT anymore</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Don't worry, battery's out right now.  Besides, I told you 61,998 miles and that's what it'll have when you get it, plus the distance from the garage to the trailer! (I know you were kidding)<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||RedOktober||12-13-2000||12:13 AM||||this one sold for a bit under $45,000, and it was an 86 SE...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  rebuilt fron nose to tail.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/512c.gif"> <P><BR>IMHO.. take the car to CA.. you'll miss her when she's gone.<BR><P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV "Red Oktober"<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater) "Blackie"<BR>87 GT "Blue Flame"||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||12-13-2000||12:42 AM||||i think since they're kind of interesting to work on after a while you want to pay less for them initially and work and/or put more money into them yourself.<P>i paid $1900 for my '87 about 3 years ago no work needed initally (clutch and head work done over the time i've had it). last spring i paid $1300 for an indy that needs work but i guess still seems worth the price. about 3-4 months ago bought an '84 for $125. changed the fuel pump and tank, alt and battery. a brake job and a few other minor (i hope ) things will still put me under $500.<P>about 2 months ago i missed out on an '86 gt that had a bad engine but included an extra good one for $650. for me i'd rather buy that one and put $2-3000 into it than buy one for $3500.<P>you'll almost have to find that certain person that is interested in fieros but not yet "into" them.  <P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||DXR_DAD||12-13-2000||01:16 AM||||LemonSky, bring it out here to Cali. we need more FieroNs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. and if you still want to sell it the folks out here have lots of $$$$<BR>for toys.<P>I sold this one for $2,500 just as you see it<BR>122,000 miles,  all stock 87 econo, factory blue, everything worked!!:<P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723597&p=17295112&Sequence=4"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723597&p=17295106&Sequence=4"> <P>Now I know nobody on the forum would pay that much..........but that doesn't mean nobody will.<P>Philphine is right you just need to find the right person and not sell it because you have to. Wait and get what you want!!!<P>DXR_DAD<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000015||firstfiero||12-13-2000||10:43 AM||||     It's hard to say in your market. I saw an 88 gt with every option with only 3000 miles on it sell for $17000.00. on the other hand a guy stoped in my restraunt just last week with an 88 gt 10000 miles on it and only one small scratch on a headlight cover and he had just bought it for 2400.00. its an odd market out their.      86 se ||||reg||1
Z||000016||elfiero||12-13-2000||11:51 AM||||IMHO-I will never pay more than $350 for a<BR>notchback,$1200 for a fastback and I won't<BR>touch any fiero with type of damage other<BR>than cosmetic or normal wear.||||reg||6
Z||000017||firstfiero||12-13-2000||12:09 PM||||  Tell me where I can get a decent fastback for 1200 and I'm their. I got my 86 se for 500 but it has some cosmetic problems and i bought it from a friend.   86se||||reg||1
Z||000018||wkayl||12-13-2000||12:33 PM||||In March or April I will be looking for one of those $1500 or less Fieros in "very good condition" within 800 miles of me (daughter getting her learners permit).  Everyone keep me in mind.  Requirements are automatic, powerdoors and windows and working A/C.||||reg||1
Z||000019||MooCow||12-13-2000||02:23 PM||||I am buying a car from Ed Parks and I am paying around $4,600 for it.  It is going to be an 87 GT metalic red with grey ground effects.  It will have a 95 Camaro engine with a 84 4-speed stick with the 4:10 gear ratio.  I am going down there in the next month to purchase it as soon as he finishes it.  So yes I would pay over $3,000 for a Fiero if it was one I wanted.  I say bring it with you cause I just sold me 86 SE and I already miss it.<P>                           Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000020||fieroparts.com||12-13-2000||02:34 PM||||I got my 1988 Fiero GT with all options<BR>inc. T-tops, leather seats, 5speed<BR>med red, gray int. With only 117 MILES that's right 117 MILES from Mrfiero.  God love that guy. For $16,000.00 and worth every<BR>penny. Went out in October to trailer<BR>it back to Maine and had a blast!<BR>If you want a low mile (NEW FIERO) see<BR>MIKE  ||||reg||14
Z||000021||overlord||12-13-2000||02:51 PM||||man, fieros must be cheap in the states. I bought  my 84 coupe for $2200 canadian (3400 american) with 90k (56k miles i think) kilometers on it.||||reg||1
Z||000022||82-T/A||12-13-2000||03:14 PM||||I sold a 1986 Fiero SE / V6 solid top for $4,200 bucks.<P>It had 52k original miles on it.<P>It was a very clean car.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000023||286SE'S||12-13-2000||04:12 PM||||When you get to California you will be able to replace that car with a nice 86 SE v6 (rust free, sun faded) for about $2500. Unfortunatly<BR>the insurance costs will probably steer you toward another car. Be sure to check those premiums BEFORE you buy another Fiero out here. If insurance wasn't so outrageous I would have a lot more than two of them.||||reg||1
Z||000024||terryk||12-13-2000||05:32 PM||||Insurance costs? $230 year? They aren't considered performance cars. <P>Here in California, Fiero's are everywhere so their values are pretty low.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-13-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000025||btoth||12-13-2000||06:03 PM||||I purchased my '86 GT for $3000 with faded paint, cracked quarter windows and really scratched up glass that I haven't replaced yet.  (It does have the factory leather seats though.)  The car needed to have the engine rebuilt 4 months after I got it and since then i've replaced just about everything.  But, I don't regret spending the $3000 on it at all.  My first car ('87 coupe) was a steal at $700.  I totaled it and the insurance company gave me $2000 for it.  That's what i get for paying $160/month.<P>I know a guy that will give me $4000 for my car just like it is (messed up paint and all) just because he likes it.  (He owns an 88 coupe).  I'd try to sell it for what you want to get for it.  You never know who will want to buy it.  And if no one looks at it, just lower it a bit.<P>Side note:  some guy my dad works with collects Fieros... he has an '88 Yellow GT with 9,000 miles on it, and has all the extras.  But, he won't part with it for less than $20,000!!<P>Sorry so long.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000026||MachineHead||12-13-2000||06:59 PM||||Whoops, never mind this.<p>[This message has been edited by MachineHead (edited 12-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||MachineHead||12-13-2000||07:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Insurance costs? $230 year? They aren't considered performance cars. <P>Here in California, Fiero's are everywhere so their values are pretty low.<P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-13-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>$230 a year?  In Jersey, the cheapest I'll ever get insurance for is $1600 a year.  Gosh darn it.||||reg||1
Z||000028||sqoach||12-13-2000||09:28 PM||||damn. there's some strange people in this forum. not naming any, cuz i wouldn't want to offend anyone. newayz...<BR>i paid $1650 for my car that i have now. when i bought it i thought it was an ok deal, but now i think i might have paid a little much. only problems it had was passenger headlight motor and the e-brake didn't work (still doesn't).<BR>i'm paying $2000 for an 86 se with a rebuilt v6. needs new headlight motors, or they need to be fixed. probably needs a new front nose, but not sure yet.<BR>i do, however, think 3500 is a little on the high side. but, like some of them said, some people will pay what you want. remember, it's only worth what someone else will pay for it, not what you think it's worth.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>fiero7@hotmail.com<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||11
Z||000029||rodmcneill||12-13-2000||11:33 PM||||A lot of people are mentioning how little they have paid for their Fiero but no mention of the time, cost or frustration in bringing back a vehicle that has over 100,000 miles to acceptable condition. I personally would never buy a vehicle with that kind of mileage.Besides that much wear on engine,trans.,clutch you also have a front end that needs rebuilt,starter, alternator,wheel bearings,brakes,headlight motors,door hinges,seats,carpet,headliner, all switches,steering column,brake cables and also exterior finish. These are just a few of the parts that seem to come up repeatdly in the forum. I would rather drive my vehicle than work on it. Now this is not to say that you cannot find a vehicle that is a good deal every once in awhile but the Fiero's I have seen around at $500-$1500 I would not touch with a ten foot pole. As a parts car maybe, but remember that old adage "you get what you pay for".||||reg||4
Z||000030||fiero56||12-14-2000||12:08 AM||||My SE is in the best mechanical condition I have ever seen a Fiero in, other than WTIII's low mile '85GT. It's 2.8 is the second strongest I have ever driven(next to WTIII's '85GT), the exhaust is the best I have ever heard, and I love the color. The paint was vandalized so it has some scratches on the passenger side of the hood.<P>My Formula was in perfect cosmetic and mechanichal condition when I bought it. But as every car I have ever seen hit the 150K mark, ball joints, shocks, engine, etc do tend to finally wear out. I don't regret buying either of my Fieros, and knowing what they have been like, I wouldn't hesitate to do the process all over again.<P><B>I would rather drive my vehicle than work on it. </B> I actually find working on my Fiero almost as enjoyable as driving it, sometimes more.||||reg||1
Z||000031||wkayl||12-14-2000||01:18 AM||||I paid $3500 for my 86 GT in June of 99.  It had 75,000 miles.  It now has 92,000 miles.  I have had to replace the alt, coil, and radio in that time.  I only had to wash and wax it.  I took 1st place in a car show in NE S.Dak (1700 mile round-trip).  Here are some pictures of it.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fierowayne.homestead.com/dadsgt.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierowayne.homestead.com/dadsgt.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000032||mrfixit58||12-14-2000||11:14 AM||||Let me see if I can out this into perspective: You want $3500 for a car whos value in the Kelly Blue Book is only $2360. The motor has 100,000 miles on it and the car has 200,000. And, in your own words, "if I have to, I'd love to take it to California. But I'm uncomfortable about driving it that kind of distance". Which, I  estimate to be only around 3,000 miles. Hummm... Now... what was the question again???<P>Remember, sentimental value is not something for which someone ELSE is likely to pay.<P>Sorry... too much $$$,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000033||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||12-16-2000||08:06 PM||||I am going to get a 1984 econo Fiero for $1000 tomorrow.  It's white with A/C, auto, 4cyl, power windows and locks, but no sunroof.  One headlight is jammed in the up position, but that's about all.  The car is a "one-family" with 116,000 total miles.  The engine was rebuilt with less than 5K miles on it since.  <BR>I am buying a first year car because I actually like it better than the pointy nosed cars that everybody else seems to want.  There's so much traffic out here in California (San Jose) that a V6 really doesn't make a difference anyway.  I rarely get to go over 30mph on the freeway ever.  We don't have rush hour, it's rush all day.  Ironic term really 'cause no one is going anywhere fast.  30 mpg will be nice with only 10 gallons of gas to guzzle.<BR>I fell in love with the Fieros when they first hit the market.  For me, It's about the look and feel, not owning a poor man's Corvette.  I have paid the traffic court way too much over the years and I am happy to drive 4 cylider automatics now.  I have one of those "bullet-proof" 1994 Ford Ranger 4cyl automatics and I love it more than the 1993 Z24 I traded in for it.  That year of the Ranger is built like a Mercedes, maybe better.  And they are cheap (&lt;$4,000 right now)<BR>So please let me say "Thanks" to all of the people who are looking for 1988 GTs.  You made my dream cheap and accessable.  I pay $250 per month to heat my house (don't even think about moving here!).  And, I am getting the car of my dreams for only $1000 bucks.  It's hard to believe.  <BR>I look forward to working on the car.  In fact, it's the only reason I am buying it.  I want to keep one of these "baby Deloreans" alive.  <BR>After 10 years in Bay area traffic I have learned that "If you can't go, then enjoy the show."  Besides, I don't know anyone who owns one.  You can't say that about a Boxster or Z3 around here, they are everywhere.<BR>I'll bet I can get the car back to nice condition for less than $5k total investment.  Then I am keeping my first year, soon to be classic car!||||reg||1
